  "summary": "Recent preprints resolve senescence by cell type and tissue position in aging liver and visceral adipose, with perturbation-linked evidence.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is senescence in aging tissues resolved at cell-type and spatial scale. In human omental visceral adipose, older adults show more senescent cells than obesity alone explains, with senescent stem/progenitor and mesothelial cells participating in multicellular signaling that drives inflammation. Liver studies extend this: senescent SenSpots localize to portal tracts and fibrotic septa in human metabolic liver disease, and senescent cells cluster in spatial inflammatory niches with distinct epigenetic senotypes in aged mouse liver. In aged mouse liver, p53 loss increases inflammation and metabolic damage, and MDM2 inhibitor-mediated p53 activation is reported to reverse age-linked changes. The chronic pancreatitis preprint, which concerns CD73 in metaplastic epithelium, is a related but distinct context. What is new is the combination of cell-type resolution, spatial context, and perturbation-linked evidence.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-21"
}
